# MCP Policy: Client/Server State

Treat filesystem authority and session identity as explicit protocol concerns.

## Rules

- Server code operates on the server filesystem; do not assume it can read client project files through ambient paths or the server working directory.
- Receive client-owned file content and metadata through the relevant protocol tool or request instead of reading a presumed local path.
- Design request handling to work without ambient session state.
- When continuity is required, use explicit client-provided session identity and bind it only through the established request-context mechanism.
- Do not infer or reuse a session from an unrelated request, process, or path.

## Rationale

Modern MCP clients may be stateless, remote, or multiplexed. Explicit authority and identity prevent accidental cross-client state and filesystem assumptions.
